What is Provisioning ?

Provisioning is the automatic creation of Cloud PCs for your end users.

After setting up Cloud PC support in MS Intune, a Cloud PC is automatically provisioned whenever a user is assigned a Cloud PC license to an appropriate MS Entra user group.

To set up Cloud PC support we can use Microsoft Hosted Network (MHN) or create Azure Network Connections (ANC).

  ANC is/are links between Cloud PCs and your on-premise resources.

We can choose a built-in Windows image (Gallery Image) or create our own image (Golden Image) to use for each Cloud PC.

You can run one Cloud PC with one license. Only one user can access Cloud PC, but it gives admin 10 minutes windows to take a session.

Billing

Cloud PCs are billed in a per-user per-month cost model.

Note : Cloud PCs are either:

Joined to your enterprise Active Directory domain and synced to Microsoft Entra ID.

Directly joined to Microsoft Entra ID.

W365 Business

W365 Business is a version of W365 made specifically for use in smaller companies (up to 300 seats). It offers an easy, streamlined way of provisioning Cloud PCs to your users.

W365 Business Downsides

It is limited up to 300 seats.

Business Cloud PCs cannot be managed through MS Intune.

You cannot configure Cloud PC for hybrid scenario (Hybrid Join).

No support for custom image.

Provisioning policies can take up to 2 hours for creating Cloud PCs.

A user will get 7 days of grace period to reassign user the license.

In case license is not reassigned the Cloud PC will automatically be deleted.

In grace period user can access Cloud PC without any problem.

Provisioning Policy path:

Home > Devices > Provision Cloud PC > Create Policy

ANC (Azure Network Connection)

Azure Network Connections (ANC) let you provision Cloud PCs that are attached to a virtual network that you manage.

You can have up to 50 ANCs per tenant.

Path to create ANC:

Devices > Cloud PC overview > Provision Cloud PCs > ANC > Manage W365 Cloud PCs

As part of connection process, the Windows 365 service is granted the following permissions:

Reader permission on Azure subscription.

W365 Network Interface Contributor role on specified resource group.

W365 Network User role on Vnet.

Requirements

Have the Intune Administrator or W365 Administrator role.

Have an AD user account with sufficient permissions to join the AD domain into the organizational unit.

To create the first ANC, you must have the subscription owner or user administrator role in the subscription where the Azure Vnet resides.

For any subsequent ANCs, only the subscription reader role is required.

For Disaster Recovery (DR) purposes, make sure that there are at least 50% of the IP addresses available in your subnet.

Make sure that your PowerShell execution policy is configured to allow RemoteSigned scripts.

  If you use Group Policy, make sure that the Group Policy Object (GPO) targeted to Organizational Unit (OU) defined in the ANC is configured to allow RemoteSigned scripts.

Windows 365 Frontline

Windows 365 Frontline is a version of Windows 365 that helps organizations save costs by letting them provision a Cloud PC that can be used by multiple users with a single license.

Windows 365 Frontline has two different modes: Dedicated mode and Shared mode.

Windows 365 in Dedicated mode

A single license: lets you provision up to three Cloud PCs that can be used non-concurrently, each assigned to a single user.

Provides one concurrent session.

Use cases:

Windows 365 Frontline dedicated mode is designed specifically for Windows workers who need a dedicated Cloud PC but don't need 24/7 access.

Frontline Cloud PCs in dedicated mode can be helpful for users who are:

On a rotation schedule.

Working across time zones and regions.

Part-time workers.

Contingent staff.

The maximum number of active Windows 365 Frontline Cloud PC sessions in your organization is equal to the number of Windows 365 Frontline licenses that you purchased.

For example, if you purchase 10 licenses, up to 30 Cloud PCs can be provisioned in dedicated mode, but only 10 of those Cloud PCs can be active at a given time.

The active sessions are managed automatically. When a user signs off from their Cloud PC, the session is released for another user to start using their Cloud PC.

Includes a concurrency buffer, to let a tenant temporarily exceed the maximum concurrency limit for Windows 365 Frontline Cloud PCs.

The concurrency buffer can be used up to four times per day with a maximum of one hour on each instance.

This hour starts from the moment the tenant exceeded the max concurrency limit.

Temporary blocks

Excessive use of the concurrency buffer temporarily blocks its further use for the next 48 hours. It is imposed when four or more occasions within a 24-hour period the concurrency buffer is used for more than one hour.

Permanent blocks

If the tenant is temporarily blocked more than two times in a seven-day period, the tenant is permanently blocked from using the concurrency buffer.

While temporarily or permanently blocked, you can still use your Frontline Cloud PCs up to the maximum concurrency limit.

Maximum concurrency limit is 1.

Notes:

W365 Frontline Cloud PCs in dedicated mode automatically powers off after the user signs off from the Cloud PC and is powered on when the user attempts to connect.

W365 Frontline in Shared mode

A single license:

Lets you provision one Cloud PC that can be shared non-concurrently among a group of users.

Provides one concurrent session.

Windows 365 Frontline in shared mode is designed specifically for workers who:

Require access to a Cloud PC to perform specialized tasks for a short time during their workday.

Don't require data persistence.

Use cases:

Customer-facing workers.

External contractors.

The maximum number of active W365 Frontline shared Cloud PC sessions in your organization is equal to the number of W365 Frontline shared licenses that you setup for a specific group.

If you assign 10 Windows 365 licenses shared, 10 Cloud PCs can be provisioned for the group.

Only a single user can connect to a shared Cloud PC at a given time.

When a user signs out from the Cloud PC, all user data is deleted and the Cloud PC is released for another user to start using.

Concurrency buffer doesn't exist for a Frontline Cloud PC on Shared mode.

With Frontline Cloud PCs in shared mode, you can also provide Cloud Apps to users instead of the full Cloud PC desktop experience.

Note:

When a tenant has both dedicated mode and shared mode provisioning policies and additional licenses are added, dedicated mode Cloud PCs are provisioned first.

Features not supported: Windows 365 Frontline

The following features aren't yet supported for W365 Frontline:

Resize a Cloud PC remote action.

Cross region disaster recovery.

It is only available for Azure Global Cloud.

Note: You can confirm your license quantities under Billing -> Your Products.

